{"availability":"available","full":"w3c/longtasks","name":"longtasks","description":"Long Task API","category":"Productivity","language":"Bikeshed","stars":249,"forks":23,"created":"2016-07-25T22:54:37Z","updated":"2026-03-19T18:51:19Z","license":null,"branch":"main","homepage":"https://w3c.github.io/longtasks/","topics":["performance","specification","webapp"],"demo":"https://longtasks.glitch.me/render-jank-demo.html","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T20:11:59.669Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-07T20:11:59.669Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-07T20:11:59.669Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-05T19:06:46.607Z","stars":249,"forks":23},{"at":"2026-10-07T20:11:59.669Z","stars":249,"forks":23}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Long Tasks is a new real user measurement (RUM) performance API to enable applications to measure responsiveness. It enables detecting presence of “long tasks” that monopolize the UI thread for extended periods of time and block other critical tasks from being executed - e.g. reacting to user input.","This API will show toplevel long tasks along with attribution for specific sub-tasks which were problematic. For instance, within a 50ms toplevel task, sub-tasks such as a 20ms script execution or a 30ms style & layout update -- will be attributed. This raises the question -- why show the toplevel task at all? Why not only show long sub-tasks such as script, style & layout etc that are directly actionable by the user? The top level task may contain some un-attributable segments such as browser work eg. GC or browser events etc.","The rationale here is that showing the toplevel task is good for web developers, even though they will actively consume the actionable sub-tasks such as long scripts and act on them. Over time the sub-task attribution will keep expanding, making more of the long task actionable. Showing the top-level task gives developers a direct indication of main thread busy-ness, and 